  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Fri Oct 2 2026\n\nAfter a VERY rainy few days across much of the area, a much\ndrier pattern is now in-place. A few shortwaves will move \nthrough the region, but global ensembles have few, if any, \nmembers producing precipitation through the end of next week. \nThis should be good news for areas with lingering flooding and \nfor harvest operations. \n\nDrier air allowed temperatures to dip into the 40s this \nmorning, but temperatures quickly returned to mid to upper 60s \nin the afternoon under clear skies. Tonight will be a touch \nwarmer than last night as southerly winds increase ahead of an \napproaching shortwave. Saturday morning low temperatures should\nrange from the mid 40s to low 50s.\n\nContinued south to southwesterly winds and clear skies on \nSaturday will promote another very effective warmup....likely\ninto the 70s for most of the area. The NBM is on the cool side \nof guidance, likely due to warm- season bias correction carrying\nover into the the more fall-like regime we are currently in. \n\nA cold front moves through Saturday evening, resulting in a\ncooler (but not overly cold) day on Sunday. Much like today\n(Friday), high temperatures should reach the 60s to low 70s,\naccompanied by light and variable winds. \n\nTemperatures then gradually warm as we head into next week.\nHighs in the 70s and lows in the 50s will be common, which is\nslightly above our climatological normals for early October.\nRain chances remain minimal (below 10%), even into next weekend\n(October 10-11th).",
